<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">craft</ansi>

Crafting lets you combine raw materials into useful items — weapons,
armor, potions, jewelry, enchantments, and more. You need the right
skill, the right ingredients, and in most cases a crafting station.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Basic Usage ━━━</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">craft list</ansi>                    Show all known recipes.
  <ansi fg="command">craft</ansi>                         Same as craft list.
  <ansi fg="command">craft <recipe></ansi>                Start crafting.
  <ansi fg="command">craft <enchant> <item></ansi>        Enchant a specific item.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Crafting Skills ━━━</ansi>

There are eight crafting skills, each with its own station and recipes:

  <ansi fg="skill">blacksmithing</ansi>  Weapons, shields, heavy armor. Station: forge.
  <ansi fg="skill">alchemy</ansi>        Potions, salves, draughts. Station: alchemy bench.
  <ansi fg="skill">tailoring</ansi>      Cloth and leather armor, cloaks. Station: loom.
  <ansi fg="skill">cooking</ansi>        Food and meals with temporary benefits. Station: hearth.
  <ansi fg="skill">jewelcrafting</ansi>  Rings, pendants, chrysalis settings. Station: jeweler bench.
  <ansi fg="skill">enchanting</ansi>     Chrysalis enchantments bound to gear. Station: enchanting circle.
  <ansi fg="skill">woodwork</ansi>      Lumber, bows, arrows, shields, furniture.
                 Station: woodworking bench.

Blacksmithing, alchemy, tailoring, cooking and woodwork are standalone
trades. Jewelcrafting and enchanting together form the <ansi fg="yellow">artificer</ansi>
profession: jewelcrafting produces chrysalis settings that enchanting
consumes to bind living mutations into equipment.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Enchanting Targets ━━━</ansi>

When enchanting, you can specify which item to enchant by name:

  <ansi fg="command">craft keen dagger</ansi>     Enchant the dagger named "dagger".
  <ansi fg="command">craft keen 2.sword</ansi>    Enchant the second sword.

Enchanting searches both your backpack and equipped items, so
you can enchant a wielded weapon without unequipping it. If
multiple items match and you don't specify, the game shows a
numbered list to choose from.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ The Crafting Loop ━━━</ansi>

  1. <ansi fg="command">forage</ansi> or loot raw materials from the world.
  2. Find the right crafting station.
  3. <ansi fg="command">craft <recipe></ansi> to begin working.
  4. Wait for the work to complete (several rounds).
  5. Use, equip, or enchant the result.

Stay with the work. If you walk away, or are carried off, before it is
done, the half-worked materials are ruined. The recipe's tool must still be
in your hands when you finish.

Higher skill means fewer wasted materials. A seasoned crafter rarely
fails, but even masters are not perfect.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Grades ━━━</ansi>

Every weapon, piece of armour, shield and piece of jewelry you craft comes
out graded by how well the work went: <ansi fg="item-quality">crude</ansi>, standard, <ansi fg="item-quality">fine</ansi>,
<ansi fg="item-quality">superb</ansi> or <ansi fg="item-quality">pristine</ansi>. Good materials and tools raise the ceiling.
Goods bought from a shop have lost their grade and count as standard, so
the finest work needs fine or better materials you gathered yourself.

                 damage   speed   weight   protection
  crude          -15%     -15%    +5%      -15%
  fine           +8%      +5%     -3%      +8%
  superb         +16%     +10%    -6%      +16%
  pristine       +25%     +15%    -10%     +25%

Damage and speed apply to weapons; protection (physical, magical and
conviction mitigation, and a shield's block) to armour and shields;
weight to all of them.

A bow or a bundle of arrows also names its wood, as in a longbow (yew).
Each wood has its own character (<ansi fg="command">help woodwork</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Starting a Craft ━━━</ansi>

Crafting takes several rounds. You cannot craft while in combat. If
attacked mid-craft, your work is interrupted (ingredients not consumed).
On completion, your skill determines the outcome. Ingredients are
consumed on any completed attempt — success or failure.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Recipe Discovery ━━━</ansi>

You begin knowing only basic recipes. New recipes reveal themselves
as you practice — each successful craft has a chance to spark a new
idea. The more you know, the harder new discoveries come.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Crafting Stations ━━━</ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">forge</ansi>              Smithing station — in Pothole Coulee and most towns.
  <ansi fg="yellow">alchemy_bench</ansi>      Alchemy station — in Pothole Coulee and most towns.
  <ansi fg="yellow">loom</ansi>               Tailoring station — in Pothole Coulee and most towns.
  <ansi fg="yellow">hearth</ansi>             Kitchen or tavern hearth.
  <ansi fg="yellow">jeweler_bench</ansi>      Jeweler's Workshop — Thornwall City.
  <ansi fg="yellow">enchanting_circle</ansi>  Enchanter's Circle — east of Jeweler's Workshop.
  <ansi fg="yellow">woodworking_bench</ansi>  Woodwork: Amber Valley, Kilnreach, Cascade Pass,
                     North Road, Pothole Coulee.
  <ansi fg="yellow">tanning_rack</ansi>       Tanning (tailoring): New Plymouth, Stillwater,
                     Kilnreach, Amber Valley.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Getting Ingredients ━━━</ansi>

Materials come from many sources:

  <ansi fg="command">forage</ansi>     Search outdoor areas for herbs, ore, stones, gems.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Mob drops</ansi>  Kill creatures that carry raw materials.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Vendors</ansi>    Some NPCs sell crafting supplies.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Floor</ansi>      Check crafting station rooms for stock.

